========================================================================================
 the TLS-noise searches: TLS handshake log noise, in SPL
========================================================================================
 The question these answer: TLS handshake errors are flooding the log pipeline and
 inflating SIEM ingest cost. Nobody has quantified the VOLUME or identified the SOURCE.
 And the operational question underneath it is a fork:

     a misconfigured load balancer health check   -> fix the health check, done
     an actual port scan                          -> a security response

 Those look identical in a raw error count. They are trivially separable once you look
 at per-source-IP timing, because a health checker is regular and a scanner is not.

 STATUS: parse-checked only. The SPL is syntactically valid, but NOTHING here has been
 executed against data, because the operational-log source it needs did not exist in the
 test environment. Every other file in this repo has been run. This one has not, and the
 reason is the section immediately below: these searches do not read the audit log.


========================================================================================
 READ THIS BEFORE YOU RUN ANYTHING: this is NOT the audit log
========================================================================================
 Every other search in this repo reads index=vault_audit sourcetype=vault:audit.
 THESE DO NOT, AND THEY CANNOT.

 The audit device records authenticated requests that REACHED Vault and were processed.
 A TLS handshake fails before any of that happens. There is no request, no token, no
 audit entry. The error is written by the server's HTTP listener to the operational
 log (stdout / syslog / journald), which is a completely different stream.

     operational log   TLS handshake errors live here    <- what the TLS-noise searches needs
     audit device      authenticated requests only       <- everything else in this repo

 Point an the TLS-noise searches panel at index=vault_audit and it will render perfectly and show zero,
 forever, with no error. That is the worst possible failure mode: a dashboard that looks
 healthy because it is looking in a place the data can never be.

 So these searches use a PLACEHOLDER source that you must replace:

     index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server

 Substitute whatever index and sourcetype your log shipper writes the Vault server's
 stdout/syslog into. If your pipeline forwards only the audit device today, then the
 operational stream is not in the SIEM at all and no query will fix that; the shipper
 config has to tee it first.

 A useful side effect: because the operational stream is separate, filtering the noise
 out of it at the forwarder does not touch the audit trail, and therefore does not
 affect audit or compliance retention. See 8.6.

 Also note these searches match on raw text, not parsed fields. Operational log format
 varies by Vault version and by whether the server is running in JSON log mode. Confirm
 with 8.0 that your lines actually contain these strings before trusting any count.
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.0  Does the operational stream exist, and what do the error lines look like?
       RUN THIS FIRST. If this returns nothing, you have not found the operational log,
       and every panel below will report a clean zero that means "never looked".
       Do not proceed on a zero here. Fix the source first.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    ("TLS handshake error" OR "tls:")
| head 20
| table _time host _raw

/*  Two error strings matter, and they mean different things:

      "http: TLS handshake error from <ip>:<port>: ..."
          the general form; the tail says why

      "tls: first record does not look like a TLS handshake"
          the specific one that means a PLAIN TCP connection hit the TLS port. This is
          the signature of a TCP-mode health check, or of a scanner banner-grabbing.
          It is not a certificate problem and no cert change will make it stop.

    Other tails you will see, which are NOT the same finding: "remote error: tls: bad
    certificate" (a real client with the wrong cert), "EOF" (a connection dropped
    mid-handshake), "client offered only unsupported versions" (an old client).
    Separate them in 8.2 rather than lumping them together.                          */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.1  VOLUME: how many, over time, per host
       The number to put in front of whoever owns the ingest bill.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    "TLS handshake error"
| timechart span=1h count BY host

/*  A dead flat line per host is itself a finding. Genuine client errors are bursty and
    follow deployments. A constant rate is machinery: health checks, or a scanner on a
    cron. Flatness alone nearly answers the question before any of the analysis below. */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.2  Break the errors out by reason
       Because the responses are different. A flood of "does not look like a TLS
       handshake" is a config fix. A flood of "bad certificate" is a client that is
       genuinely trying and failing, and suppressing that one hides a real outage.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    ("TLS handshake error" OR "tls:")
| rex field=_raw "TLS handshake error from (?<src_ip>[0-9a-fA-F\.:]+):(?<src_port>\d+)"
| eval reason = case(
      match(_raw, "first record does not look like a TLS handshake"), "plaintext on TLS port",
      match(_raw, "bad certificate"),                                 "client cert rejected",
      match(_raw, "unknown certificate"),                             "client rejected our cert",
      match(_raw, "unsupported versions"),                            "obsolete TLS version",
      match(_raw, "EOF"),                                             "connection dropped mid-handshake",
      match(_raw, "TLS handshake error"),                             "other handshake error",
      true(),                                                          "unclassified")
| stats count AS errors, dc(src_ip) AS distinct_sources, dc(host) AS vault_nodes
        BY reason
| eventstats sum(errors) AS total
| eval share_pct = round(100 * errors / total, 1)
| fields reason errors share_pct distinct_sources vault_nodes
| sort - errors

/*  The rex is the load-bearing line in this whole file. If distinct_sources comes back
    null or 0 while errors is large, the regex did not match your log format and every
    per-IP panel below is empty for that reason and not because there are no sources.
    Check a raw line from 8.0 and adjust the pattern. IPv6 sources are why the
    character class includes ':'; a v6 address plus port needs a different anchor, so
    if your listener is dual-stack, verify against a real v6 line.                     */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.3  ATTRIBUTION: which source IPs, and how do they behave
       Raw counts per IP are not enough to answer the fork, so this measures the
       BEHAVIOUR of each source alongside its volume.

       The discriminator is interval regularity. A health checker fires on a timer, so
       the gaps between its connections are near identical and the stdev of those gaps
       is tiny. A human or a scanning tool produces irregular gaps.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    "TLS handshake error"
| rex field=_raw "TLS handshake error from (?<src_ip>[0-9a-fA-F\.:]+):(?<src_port>\d+)"
| where isnotnull(src_ip)
| sort 0 src_ip _time
| streamstats current=f last(_time) AS prev_time BY src_ip
| eval gap = _time - prev_time
| stats count AS errors,
        avg(gap) AS avg_gap, stdev(gap) AS gap_stdev,
        dc(host) AS vault_nodes_hit,
        dc(src_port) AS distinct_src_ports,
        min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen
        BY src_ip
| eval duration_h = round((last_seen - first_seen)/3600, 1),
       avg_gap    = round(avg_gap, 1),
       gap_stdev  = round(gap_stdev, 1)

  ``` coefficient of variation: stdev relative to the mean. Near 0 is a metronome. ```
| eval gap_cv = if(avg_gap > 0, round(gap_stdev / avg_gap, 2), null())

| eval first_seen = strftime(first_seen,"%F %T"), last_seen = strftime(last_seen,"%F %T")
| table src_ip errors avg_gap gap_cv duration_h vault_nodes_hit distinct_src_ports first_seen last_seen
| sort - errors
| head 50

/*  streamstats needs the events ordered by IP then time, hence the explicit
    `sort 0 src_ip _time`. The 0 means no result limit; the default sort caps at 10000
    rows and would silently truncate a real flood.

    Reading the table:
      low gap_cv + long duration_h + few src_ports   -> a timer. Health check.
      high gap_cv + short duration_h + many ports    -> a burst. Scan.               */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.4  THE PANEL: separate the two populations
       8.3 gives a human the evidence. This one makes the call, so the dashboard says
       what to do rather than making on-call derive it at 3am.

       Thresholds here are starting values, not physics. Tune them against 8.3 output
       from your own estate before wiring anything to an alert.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    "TLS handshake error"
| rex field=_raw "TLS handshake error from (?<src_ip>[0-9a-fA-F\.:]+):(?<src_port>\d+)"
| where isnotnull(src_ip)
| sort 0 src_ip _time
| streamstats current=f last(_time) AS prev_time BY src_ip
| eval gap = _time - prev_time
| stats count AS errors,
        avg(gap) AS avg_gap, stdev(gap) AS gap_stdev,
        dc(src_port) AS distinct_src_ports,
        dc(host) AS vault_nodes_hit,
        min(_time) AS first_seen, max(_time) AS last_seen
        BY src_ip
| eval duration_h = (last_seen - first_seen)/3600,
       gap_cv     = if(avg_gap > 0, gap_stdev / avg_gap, 99)

| eval population = case(

      ``` metronome timing sustained over hours, from one IP, is machinery on a timer.
          This is the population that should be fixed at the load balancer, not alerted on. ```
      gap_cv < 0.25 AND duration_h > 6 AND errors > 20, "HEALTH CHECKER (fix the config)",

      ``` many source ports in a short window is the shape of a port sweep or a
          connection flood. One client reconnecting normally does not do this. ```
      distinct_src_ports > 100 AND duration_h < 1, "SCAN (investigate)",

      ``` irregular and short lived. Not a timer, not obviously a sweep either. ```
      gap_cv >= 1 AND duration_h < 2, "BURSTY (investigate)",

      true(), "UNCLASSIFIED (eyeball it)")

| eval avg_gap = round(avg_gap,1), gap_cv = round(gap_cv,2), duration_h = round(duration_h,1)
| stats sum(errors) AS errors, dc(src_ip) AS source_ips, values(src_ip) AS ips BY population
| eventstats sum(errors) AS total
| eval share_pct = round(100 * errors / total, 1)
| fields population errors share_pct source_ips ips
| sort - errors

/*  Deliberately conservative. UNCLASSIFIED is a legitimate output and it is better than
    a confident wrong label, because the two labels here lead to opposite responses and
    calling a scan a health check is the expensive mistake.

    A single IP does not have to be one thing forever. A load balancer that is ALSO
    being scanned shows up as one IP with a mixed profile, which lands in UNCLASSIFIED,
    which is the correct place for it.

    `values(src_ip)` will be truncated at 100 by default. Fine for HEALTH CHECKER, which
    is a small stable set by definition. For SCAN drop the ips column and use 8.3.    */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.5  COST: what share of ingest is this noise
       The panel that justifies doing anything about it. Two forms below.

       Form A uses the license usage index, which is the authoritative number but needs
       access to _internal. Form B estimates from line size and needs no special access.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Form A: measured against actual licensed ingest volume
index=_internal source=*license_usage.log type=Usage
    earliest=-7d@d latest=@d
| stats sum(b) AS bytes BY st
| eventstats sum(bytes) AS total_bytes
| eval gb = round(bytes/1024/1024/1024, 2),
       share_pct = round(100 * bytes / total_bytes, 2)
| sort - bytes
| head 20

/*  Locate your operational sourcetype in that list to see what the whole stream costs.
    Then Form B tells you what fraction of it is handshake noise.                      */


 Form B: estimate the noise share from the line sizes themselves
index=vault_operational sourcetype=vault:server
    earliest=-7d@d latest=@d
| eval is_tls_noise = if(match(_raw, "TLS handshake error"), 1, 0)
| eval bytes = len(_raw)
| stats count AS events, sum(bytes) AS bytes BY is_tls_noise
| eventstats sum(events) AS total_events, sum(bytes) AS total_bytes
| eval category   = if(is_tls_noise==1, "TLS handshake noise", "everything else"),
       gb_7d      = round(bytes/1024/1024/1024, 3),
       gb_per_day = round(bytes/1024/1024/1024/7, 3),
       gb_year    = round(bytes/1024/1024/1024/7*365, 1),
       pct_events = round(100 * events / total_events, 1),
       pct_bytes  = round(100 * bytes / total_bytes, 1)
| fields category events pct_events gb_7d gb_per_day gb_year pct_bytes
| sort - gb_7d

/*  len(_raw) is the uncompressed line length, which is the right basis: Splunk licenses
    on raw ingested volume. It still UNDERSTATES the real cost, because it ignores
    per-event index overhead and the storage the events occupy for the whole retention
    period, not just the day they arrive. Treat gb_year as a floor.

    To turn it into currency, multiply gb_year by your own per-GB rate. No rate is
    hardcoded here, because that number is different for every deployment and a stale
    hardcoded price in a public repo is worse than no price.                          */


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8.6  What to actually do about it
       Not a search. The searches above quantify a problem; this is the fix, and the
       order matters.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*  1. FIX THE SOURCE FIRST, if 8.4 says HEALTH CHECKER.
       "plaintext on TLS port" from a load balancer means the health check is doing a
       bare TCP connect against an HTTPS listener. Vault sees an unencrypted byte
       stream on a TLS port and logs an error, correctly. Point the health check at
       HTTPS and Vault's own health endpoint instead, and the errors stop at the
       source, which costs nothing to run and needs no filter to maintain.

       When you do, treat BOTH of these as healthy: the active node and a standby node
       answer that endpoint with different status codes, and a health check that accepts
       only one of them will mark healthy standbys as down.

    2. FILTER AT THE FORWARDER ONLY IF YOU CANNOT FIX THE SOURCE.
       Dropping at the log shipper (a grep/exclude filter on the operational stream)
       means the events never become billable. Dropping in Splunk at index time via
       nullQueue does NOT: the data has already crossed the license meter.

       Two cautions. Filtering is permanent for those events, so route them to a
       cheap long-retention destination instead of deleting outright if you might ever
       need them for forensics. And match narrowly on the specific handshake string,
       never on "tls", or you will also drop the certificate expiry warnings that are
       the ones you genuinely want.

    3. DO NOT FILTER IF 8.4 SAYS SCAN.
       Suppressing the log of an active scan is deleting the evidence. Route it to a
       security index, do not drop it.

    4. THE AUDIT TRAIL IS NOT AFFECTED, EITHER WAY.
       All of this is the operational stream. The audit device is a separate output and
       nothing here touches it, so audit and compliance retention are unchanged.
       Worth saying out loud to whoever has to approve the filter, because "we are
       going to stop logging some errors" sounds much worse than it is.               */
